# DFA Simulator

Design, visualize, and simulate Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) in a dark, clean UI with smooth, speed-aware animations.

Author: Nilkantha Dwibedi
Live Link: https://nil-frontend.github.io/DFA-Simulator/

## Overview
- Define DFA components: states, alphabet, start state, accepting states, and transitions
- Render an interactive graph of the DFA
- Simulate input strings step-by-step with animated state and edge highlights
- Control playback with Start, Play, Pause, Step, and Reset
- Choose speed: Slow (1000ms), Normal (600ms), Fast (300ms)
- Track input progress and see the path taken across states
- Get Accepted/Rejected result when the input finishes

## Features
- Create/Update DFA graph from user inputs
- Fit to Screen button to center/zoom the graph
- Animated transitions:
  - Current state glow and scale
  - Active transition edge highlight
- Input Progress:
  - Consumed symbols shown as animated pills
  - Remaining input displayed live
- Path Taken:
  - Sequence of visited states updated per step
  - Current state highlighted
- Deterministic, timer-based simulation with interruptible playback

## Getting Started
1. Open index.html in a modern browser (no build step required)
2. Define states, alphabet, start and accepting states
3. Set transitions (δ(state, symbol) = nextState)
4. Click Create/Update DFA Graph
5. Enter an input string and Start the simulation
6. Use Play/Pause/Step/Reset and adjust Speed as needed

## Default Example
- The default DFA accepts all strings over {0,1} that end with "01"
- Try inputs: "1101", "01", "1010"

## Tech Stack
- HTML, CSS, JavaScript
- Cytoscape.js for graph visualization

## Project Structure
- index.html — UI layout and controls
- style.css — Dark theme and animation styles
- script.js — DFA data, Cytoscape rendering, and simulation state machine

